Welcome to the Apache web server kit. This kit is designed to help understand how to query and get a start at analyzing logs generated by your Apache web servers.

This playbook serves to get you oriented around the kit and provide some anchors into the data and dashboards.

# Integration Guide
An integration guide is available on [this documentation page](https://docs.gravwell.io/integrations/application/apache.html)

# Overview

There are 2 tags associated with this kit. The primary tag ($APACHE) contains access logs, and the error tag ($APACHE_ERR) captures errors from the web server. 

Configuration macros are used in the queries to easily swap in whichever tag you choose to use. 

- $APACHE - the integration guide assumes you use tag=apache on ingester, and have configured Apache for json output
- $APACHE_ERR - the integration guide assumes you use tag=apache-err

This tag looks at an entire tag as a whole, and doesn't provide options to filter by a specific host

## Apache Connection Logs
Connection logs contain the following fields. Queries provided in the kit apply broadly to all Apache logs in your Gravwell instance. The vhost field can be used to specify which particular server you'd like to filter on when narrowing your search to specific web addresses.

- remote_addr
- forwarded_for
- method
- uri
- status
- bytes
- duration
- referer
- user_agent
- vhost

## Apache Error Logs
Error logs are assumed to be in default format. An autoextractor is configured for tag=apache-err, and can be accessed with the ax processor. This autoextractor must be updated if you use a different tag than apache-err.

```
tag=apache-err ax
```

- module
- loglevel
- pid
- client_ip
- client_port
- error_code
- message
- referer

# Queries
Queries are broken out into 2 types and have appropriate labels applied for reference. 

## Stats
- Primarily used by dashboards to genereate metrics and data that provides an overview of activity, though can be used for ad hoc searches in query studio.

## Detections
- Queries built to detect very specific behavior that is indicative of malicious activity. These can be used ad hoc, serve as a basis for your own detection engineering efforts, or enabled as scheduled searches which can be used for alerts. 

# Dashboards
3 dashboards are provided in this kit. 

## Apache - Traffic Overview
High-level statistics of connections. Volume over time, top URI, user agent frequency, top client IPs, and HTTP status code distribution.

## Apache - Attack Surface
This dashboard may indicate suspicious activity. Queries are built with lists, and likely need to be extended and customized for your purposes, but these serve as a solid starting point.
- Provides information about errors. 
- A list of user agents are configured for suspicious UA. 
- A list of suspicious URIs that indicate probing behavior.

## Apache - IP Investigation
Prompts for an IP address, and will provide information about connection requests coming from it. Will surface user agents, errors, HTTP status codes, methods, and an activity timeline.

# Actionables
## Apache - IP Lookup
A variety of actions are provided for IP addresses in Apache connection logs. These allow you to quickly pivot to searching for specific activity associated to the requesting IP

Actions:
- IP Investigation Dashboard
- IP Activity timeline
- URI and Methods
- User Agents